+## D18 — Hardware owns tiling and a simple backing-memory interface
+
+**Accepted and implemented (2026-10-15).** The user requested hardware tile
+scheduling and transfers with SRAM-like memory behavior before a physical DMA
+protocol. `conv_accelerator` owns the full-layer descriptor and tile cursors;
+its FSM performs serialized reads, local preloads, core jobs, output reads and
+stores. There is no software tile planner. The off-chip-memory model is an array
+inside `tb_conv_accelerator`, never another RTL memory module.
+
+One image and one output row per tile keep the mapping simple. Hardware clips
+actual input rectangles and adjusts local padding, preserving the original
+address generator's zero injection. A per-tap fallback covers kernels or
+minimum dilated footprints exceeding SRAM. Input/output/kernel capacities are
+independent; output SRAM retains sums until all reduction blocks complete.
+Existing PE weight double buffering remains active within a job. The new core
+continuation flag defaults to 0 in the direct core bench; readback requests are
+accepted only while idle and share the existing SRAM read port.
+
+The backing port uses enabled one-cycle synchronous reads and edge writes,
+word addresses and one element per word. No backpressure, AXI or physical memory
+controller is implied. State ownership and correctness precede overlap or data
+reuse optimization. D04's direct-preload `conv_core` remains available; D12's
+single core bench is retained alongside one system bench for the new top. They
+share the strict tensor reader and existing runners. Full-layer results and
+intermediate partial sums must be checked against independent reference loops;
+only explicitly failing negative controls may force signals.
+
+Checkpoints 39/40 preserve the before/validated hardware-tiling implementation.
+Old tiled sources were neither copied nor consulted for this implementation.
